# VibeWarden Agent Kickoff Prompt (vibew v0.0.0-test)
#
# Generated by: vibew prompt-template --deploy --name foo --describe "bar" --domain demo.example.com
# Regenerate any time: vibew prompt-template --help

You are an AI coding agent setting up a new application with the VibeWarden
security sidecar. Follow each step in order. Do not skip steps or combine them.
These instructions are generated by vibew v0.0.0-test and match that exact binary.

## Step 1 — Install vibew

  npm install -g @vibewarden/cli

No Node.js? Use the shell installer instead:

  curl -fsSL https://vibewarden.dev/install.sh | sh

Verify: `vibew --version` should print a version string.

## Step 2 — Scaffold the project

NOTE: `vibew init` does not create app code or a Dockerfile. You must
provide both. AGENTS-VIBEWARDEN.md describes the contract.

  # Skip if you're already in the project directory:
  mkdir foo && cd foo
  vibew init --name foo --describe "bar"

This creates vibewarden.yaml, .gitignore, AGENTS-VIBEWARDEN.md, and AGENTS.md.
Read AGENTS-VIBEWARDEN.md — it lists security features you must NOT reimplement.

## Step 3 — Add TLS

  vibew add tls --domain demo.example.com

## Step 4 — Write your application

Write your application code. Read AGENTS-VIBEWARDEN.md before writing any auth,
rate-limiting, or security logic — VibeWarden handles those at the sidecar layer.

## Step 5 — Start the dev loop

  vibew dev

The sidecar starts on localhost. Your app is proxied through it automatically.

## Step 6 — Build the deploy bundle

When your application is ready to deploy:

  vibew bundle

This produces a self-contained deploy artifact under .vibewarden/bundle/.

IMPORTANT — architecture mismatch (Apple Silicon / ARM64 hosts):
If you are building on Apple Silicon (linux/arm64) and deploying to a standard
amd64 VPS, run this instead:

  vibew build --platform linux/amd64
  vibew bundle

vibew bundle hard-fails (exit 1) if the image arch does not match the deploy
target (default linux/amd64). The error message prints the exact rebuild command.
Override the target via --target-platform or deploy.target_platform in
vibewarden.production.yaml.

Optional pre-bundle gate: run `vibew doctor --preflight production` before
`vibew bundle` to catch DNS, port, arch, and TLS-email mistakes against the
merged production config before the build starts.

## Step 7 — Deploy to your server

NOTE: VibeWarden does not deploy for you. `vibew bundle` produces a
self-contained bundle and prints the exact ssh / scp / docker commands.
You run them with your own SSH target.

# NOTE: this is the canonical deploy recipe defined in ADR-099.
# The bundle README opens with this same fenced block (app.name and tls.domain substituted).
# Run these commands directly — no shell script wrapper.

  ssh '<your-ssh-user>@<your-ssh-host>' 'mkdir -p /opt/foo'
  tar -czf - -C .vibewarden/bundle . | ssh '<your-ssh-user>@<your-ssh-host>' 'tar -xzf - -C /opt/foo/'
  ssh '<your-ssh-user>@<your-ssh-host>' "cd /opt/foo && docker load -i image.tar && docker compose up -d"

Replace `<your-ssh-user>@<your-ssh-host>` with your actual SSH target.
  - Check `~/.ssh/config` for an existing alias.
  - Or set `deploy.host: user@host` in `vibewarden.production.yaml`
    (vibew will substitute it into the bundle stdout next time).

## Step 8 — Verify deployment

  vibew probe --env production

Expected output ends with `OK — production stack healthy.`
If `components.upstream` is `"unknown"`, the first health probe has not yet
completed — vibew probe retries automatically for up to 10s.
If it is `"failing"`, the upstream container is not accepting connections.

If you see a TLS handshake error immediately after `docker compose up -d`,
ACME (Let's Encrypt) cert issuance may still be in progress — vibew probe
retries automatically for up to 30s, printing progress to stderr. If the
cert is not issued within that window, the command exits 1 with a message
pointing at `docker compose logs vibewarden | grep -i acme`. Wait ~1 minute
and re-run `vibew probe --env production`.

Fallback (when vibew binary is not available on the operator machine):

  curl -fsSL https://demo.example.com/_vibewarden/health

Expected: HTTP 200 with JSON body `"status":"ok"`, `"components":{"upstream":"ok"}`.
